exports[`the pages say what they said > the submit wizard, adding a skill 1`] = `
{
  "spoken": [
    "title=The exchange itself is new: the category vocabulary, the metadata fields and the submission and review workflows are all still changing.",
    "aria-label=Main",
    "aria-label=Switch to dark theme",
    "aria-label=What do you want to do?",
    "placeholder=github.com/you/your-skill",
    "placeholder=---
name: my-skill
...",
    "placeholder=Permit Status Explainer",
    "placeholder=1.0",
    "placeholder=US-MA / Boston",
    "placeholder=en, es",
    "placeholder=Read, Grep",
    "placeholder=US-MA / Boston",
    "placeholder=2026-03",
  ],
  "text": "Skip to contentCivic Skill ExchangeBetaBrowseAboutSubmitGitHubDarkAgent skills for government, public-sector and nonprofit workA city that solves a problem once should be able to hand the solution to the next hundred cities.4 skills·1 reviewed·3 communityShare a skillFill this in and we will put it in the right shape for you. It takes a few minutes.You will need a GitHub account to finish — it is free, and it is what records the skill as yours. Create one if you do not have one; it takes a couple of minutes and you can come back to this page afterwards.Add a new skillUpdate one you already listedThe skill will be listed as a community skill until it is reviewed.Submit a new skillAlready have one? Drop it here and the rest of this page fills itself in. If your skill lives in its own repository, GitHub’s Code → Download ZIP gives you the file to drop.Your skill's GitHub repositoryPublic repositories only. We read the file list and SKILL.md, and hand the folder back for you to upload — the listing records where the copy came from.Read itOr upload the skill folder as a .zipUnpacked in your browser. It is not sent anywhere.Or paste your SKILL.mdAbout the skillYour GitHub usernameThis has to match your login exactly — your skill goes in a folder of that name, and only you can write there.Skill nameType it however you like; we will tidy the spacing and capitals.the skill name is requiredDescriptionWhat the skill does, in a couple of sentences. This is what an agent reads to decide whether to use it.the description is requiredCategoryChoose…PolicyData AnalysisCommunicationsFinanceHRTechnologyConstituent ServicesBenefits & EligibilityPermitting & LicensingLegalPublic Records & TransparencyOperations & Service DeliveryEmergency Management & Public SafetyPlanning & Land UseAI Toolsthe category is requiredA second category, if it fits oneThe list mixes what a skill is for with whose desk it sits on, so many skills belong in two places. Leave this alone if yours does not.None — it sits in one placePolicyData AnalysisCommunicationsFinanceHRTechnologyConstituent ServicesBenefits & EligibilityPermitting & LicensingLegalPublic Records & TransparencyOperations & Service DeliveryEmergency Management & Public SafetyPlanning & Land UseAI ToolsVersion, if you keep oneYour own number for it, like 1.0 or 2.1.3. Optional, and nothing checks it — it is there so an adopter can tell this is not what they took last year. The registry records when a skill arrived and last changed on its own.What level of government is it for?Choose…Any level of government — it makes no assumptionsA city, county or townA state, province or regionA national governmentA body above national governmentthe level of government is requiredA second level, if it serves twoNone — one levelA city, county or townA state, province or regionA national governmentA body above national governmentIs it written for one specific place?Only if the skill carries that place’s rules, forms or deadlines — US-VT, US-MA / Boston, CA-ON / Toronto. Leave it blank otherwise, which is most skills. A country code, optionally a state or province, and optionally a city after a slash.Is it set up for one place, or does it work anywhere?What this means — a localized skill carries one jurisdiction’s specifics; a generalized one has had them taken out so another city can fill in its own.Not sure yetSet up for one place — it has our forms, deadlines and rules in itWorks anywhere — the local specifics have been lifted outWhat language is it written in?The language of the SKILL.md itself. It is not a limit on who can use the skill — a model reads a skill in one language and follows it in another. It is so a reader knows what they are about to open.Choose…EnglishSpanishAnother language — I will give the tagthe language it is written in is requiredWhat data does it touch?No personal dataPersonal details about identifiable peopleHealth, benefits, immigration or criminal justice dataDoes what it produces affect anyone's rights or benefits?No — it does not affect anyone's rights or benefitsIt informs a person, but decides nothingIt feeds a decision someone acts onHave you used it?Using it yourself is a complete answer — nothing else is required.Not yet — I have not used it in real workI use it myselfMy team uses itMy whole organization uses itWho maintains it?A person or a team — City of X, Department of Innovation.who maintains it is requiredWhat kind of organization?Choose…GovernmentNonprofitVendorAcademicJust methe kind of organization is requiredA few optional thingsWhen is this useful?When is it not?The one only you can answer. A skill honest about its limits gets adopted faster.What languages have you tried it in?Comma-separated tags, including the one above — en, es. Your own claim: nothing here checks it, and the page shows it as something you said rather than something anybody verified.Tools it needsComma separated. These are granted without asking the person who runs it, so list the least it needs.LicenseWhich organization uses it?Where does that organization operate?Like US-MA / Boston.Roughly since when?Send it7 things still to fill in, marked above. You can send it anyway — the checks that count run after you do, and you can fix things then.Continue on GitHubCopy itEvery route from here goes through GitHub, so an account is required — the checks that admit a skill work by confirming the account that submitted it owns the folder it went into. If that is a problem, open an issue or ask whoever pointed you at this page; a maintainer can submit on your behalf, and the listing will credit you as the maintainer.See what will be added---
license: "MIT"
metadata:
  civic.data-sensitivity: "none"
  civic.human-review: "none"
  civic.deployment: "none"
---
Or do it from the command linegit clone https://github.com/https://example.test/repo.git
  mkdir -p skills//
  git checkout -b add-your-skill
  git commit -am "Add skills//"
  git push origin add-your-skillInclusion in this registry is not an endorsement. Automated checks can only reject — a pass is never a statement that a skill is safe.Catalog generated 8/30/2026 · Source and submissions on GitHub · About this project",
}
